# FFmpeg internals use their own lock-free synchronization that TSan
# cannot model; races wholly inside FFmpeg libraries are not ours. The
# system libraries are stripped, so symbol-scoped `race:` entries do not
# match -- called_from_lib is the only granularity that works. Known cost:
# a real Kit bug (e.g. an object freed on one thread while another still
# uses it) is also silenced whenever one of the two racing accesses happens
# to execute inside a suppressed library, with Kit code only deeper in the
# stack. To re-audit an entry, delete its line, re-run the suite, and
# symbolize the reported offsets with addr2line against the debuginfod
# debuginfo.
#
# libavcodec -- investigated 2026-07-26 (suspected Kit_PlayerStop teardown
# race, ex-TODO item): with this entry removed, every report symbolizes
# (via debuginfod) to pairs wholly inside h264 frame threading --
# ff_h264_free_tables under avcodec_flush_buffers vs ff_er_add_slice /
# h264_frame_start on av:h264 workers. FFmpeg parks workers through an
# atomic fast path (state == INPUT_READY skips the mutex), and atomics in
# the uninstrumented system library create no happens-before edge TSan can
# see, so these are false positives.
#
# libavutil / libavformat / libswscale -- audited 2026-08-02 by removing
# these entries and re-running the suite: hundreds of reports fire, and
# every racing access pair (frames #0/#1) sits inside the libraries
# themselves; Kit code appears only deeper in the stacks as the caller.
# libavutil dominates because it hosts the buffer pools and threading
# helpers that avcodec's frame-threading workers run on.
#
# libswresample -- did not fire in the 2026-08-02 audit run, but audio
# resampling exercises the same uninstrumented-atomics pattern from the
# audio decoder thread; kept on the same basis as the entries above.
called_from_lib:libavcodec
called_from_lib:libavformat
called_from_lib:libavutil
called_from_lib:libswscale
called_from_lib:libswresample

